    <a
    href="https://github.com/graphql/graphiql/commit/093cb100a4524b1005b82c1c064bb897416bfc82"><code>093cb10</code></a>
    Thanks <a
    href="https://github.com/trevor-scheer"><code>@​trevor-scheer</code></a>!
    - Add <code>createTransport</code> and a structured
    <code>Transport</code> API for queries, mutations, subscriptions, and
    incremental delivery. Pass a transport through <code>&lt;GraphiQL
    transport={...}&gt;</code>; the <code>transport</code> and
    <code>fetcher</code> props are mutually exclusive at the type level.</p>
    <p><code>GraphiQLProps</code> is now a union. If a TypeScript wrapper
    uses <code>interface AppProps extends GraphiQLProps</code>, replace it
    with a type intersection such as <code>type AppProps = GraphiQLProps
    &amp; { applicationName: string }</code>, even when retaining
    <code>fetcher</code>. See the <a
    href="https://github.com/graphql/graphiql/blob/c2e792bc446d5d6751bd7cd1d9ac283a4a1d8fc4/docs/migration/graphiql-6.0.0.md#overview">migration
    overview</a>.</p>
    <p>When <code>supportedMethods</code> is omitted,
    <code>createTransport</code> discovers endpoint capabilities through
    <code>OPTIONS</code> and the <code>Allow</code> header. Queries prefer
    QUERY, then POST, then GET. Mutations and operations of unknown type
    require POST. An explicit <code>method</code> override applies to
    queries and fails if the endpoint excludes it. GET sends GraphQL
    parameters in the URL; POST and QUERY send a JSON body.</p>
    <p>Discovery is shared by concurrent requests and repeats when effective
    request headers change. If discovery fails, exceeds two seconds, or the
    <code>Allow</code> header is missing or malformed, the transport assumes
    only its initially configured <code>method</code> (POST by default) is
    available. A valid advertisement containing no supported methods blocks
    execution. Pass a nonempty <code>supportedMethods</code> array to skip
    discovery for known capabilities. Cross-origin servers must permit
    OPTIONS and expose <code>Allow</code> through
    <code>Access-Control-Expose-Headers</code>.</p>
    <p><code>TransportResponse</code> exposes HTTP method, status, headers,
    timing, and byte sizes alongside the GraphQL result. HTTP metadata
    remains available when a response body is not valid JSON. Its
    <code>ok</code> field accounts for both HTTP status and GraphQL errors.
    <code>TransportRequest</code> supports GraphQL <code>extensions</code>
    and an abort <code>signal</code>. Subscriptions require an explicit
    <code>subscriptionClient</code> with an <code>iterate(request)</code>
    method, supported by <code>graphql-ws</code> v6 and
    <code>graphql-sse</code> clients.</p>
    <p>Plugins can transform outgoing requests with
    <code>onBeforeSend</code> and observe responses or request failures with
    <code>onResponse</code> and <code>onError</code>, available through
    <code>useGraphiQLPluginContext().transport</code>. Each registration
    returns a cleanup function. This context field is absent when using the
    legacy <code>fetcher</code> prop.</p>
    <p>Transports can expose query method preferences through optional
    <code>setMethod</code>, <code>methodOverride</code>, and
    <code>subscribe(listener)</code> APIs. Calling <code>setMethod()</code>
    without an argument restores automatic selection.
    <code>methodOverride</code> distinguishes an explicit preference from
    Auto; <code>Transport.method</code> reflects the query method, while
    <code>TransportResponse.method</code> reports the method actually used
    for an execution. <code>subscribe(listener)</code> notifies GraphiQL of
    preference and capability changes and returns an unsubscribe function.
    These notifications are independent of request lifecycle hooks.
    <code>createTransport</code> implements these APIs; custom transports
    can omit them.</p>
    <p><code>createGraphiQLFetcher</code>, <code>Fetcher</code>, and
    <code>&lt;GraphiQL fetcher={...}&gt;</code> are deprecated but remain
    supported. See the <a
    href="https://github.com/graphql/graphiql/blob/c2e792bc446d5d6751bd7cd1d9ac283a4a1d8fc4/docs/migration/graphiql-6.0.0.md#new-transport-api">Transport
    migration guide</a> for examples and custom transport requirements.</p>
